## Proof

Let
$$Y_n \;=\; \min\{X_n,\,X_{n+1},\,X_{n+2}\}.$$

Since the $X_i$ are iid $\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)$, each $X_i(\omega)\in[0,1]$ for every sample point $\omega$, and consequently
$$0 \;\le\; Y_n(\omega) \;\le\; 1 \qquad\text{for all } n \text{ and all } \omega.$$

Therefore, for every sample path,
$$0 \;\le\; \frac{\log n}{n^{1/3}}\,Y_n(\omega) \;\le\; \frac{\log n}{n^{1/3}}.$$

It is a basic fact of real analysis that polynomial growth dominates logarithmic growth: for any $\alpha>0$,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log n}{n^{\alpha}} \;=\; 0.$$
Taking $\alpha = 1/3$,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log n}{n^{1/3}} \;=\; 0.$$

Applying the squeeze theorem pathwise (the bounds $0$ and $\log n/n^{1/3}$ are both deterministic and independent of $\omega$), we obtain, for **every** sample point $\omega$,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log n}{n^{1/3}}\,\min\{X_n(\omega),X_{n+1}(\omega),X_{n+2}(\omega)\} \;=\; 0.$$

Since the convergence holds for every $\omega$ in the sample space, it holds **almost surely** (in fact, surely). A fortiori the limit is also $0$ in probability, in $L^p$ for every $p\ge 1$, and in distribution.

**Remark on the role of the minimum.** The factor $\min\{X_n,X_{n+1},X_{n+2}\}$ is bounded in $[0,1]$ regardless of $n$ — it is the minimum of only three iid uniforms, so its distribution is $\mathrm{Beta}(1,3)$ with mean $1/4$, and it does **not** shrink with $n$. (This is unlike $\min\{X_1,\dots,X_n\}$, which shrinks like $1/n$.) Hence the entire behavior of the sequence is governed by the deterministic prefactor $\log n/n^{1/3}\to 0$, which drives the product to $0$.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log n}{n^{1/3}}\min\{X_n,X_{n+1},X_{n+2}\}=0\quad\text{a.s.}}$$

